 #:seon.task{:name "a-branch-test-world-runs-the-jvm-loaded-program-not-its-fork-point-record"
             :plan "`seon.test/clean-request-handle` builds a test world's source with `(seon.sci.eval/base-ctx candidate)`, whose loaded program is `seon.sci.eval/loaded-program` of the candidate branch: the adoption record the branch copied at its fork point, not the JVM's (`seon.sci.eval/loaded-source` reads main's through `:seon.sci.eval/loaded-connection`; its docstring: a branch's own record stays behind a later adoption). After a restart onto a commit that deletes or renames contracted functions, every branch forked before it builds its test world on the old published commit: `seon.sci.eval/derive-program-context` finds no compiled Var for the old functions, interprets them against the published-source database, which holds no configuration, and each contract install refuses (`seon.sci.eval/database-effective-config`). Default recorded 20 such refusals for `seon.cluster.squash/attribute-tx` at the 10:10Z boot of b14dc2b3f, the commit after M6 renamed `seon.cluster.squash` (recorded error 500ddeeeca09, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/db-clean/root-causes.md`). Even with nothing deleted, such a world judges the branch against the old platform source instead of the compiled one. The world-base also read the branch's record (`seon.test/world-base` on the recording branch) and the overlay copied every difference from it. The world is the JVM's loaded program (`seon.sci.eval/loaded-identity`) plus the candidate's changes since the program its record names (`seon.test/program-overlay-tx`); the proof forks a branch from a commit holding a function the world lacks and records that commit as its program. Read `seon.cluster/name-loaded-program!`, `seon.sci.eval/base-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/loaded-database` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/db-clean/faults-before-reset.edn`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-effect-attempt-opens-only-for-a-test-calling-an-effect-owner"
             :plan "`seon.test/run-request` opens an attempt agent and turn (`seon.test/attempt-request-context`, one transaction, 350 to 440 ms on a member branch by its comment) for every member in `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching` of `seon.effect/request*`, which on default is 2,699 of 3,016 tests, where 21 tests reach it by `:seon.fn/calls` edges alone and `my.fs/read` is called by 16. Cause (measured on default's `seon.test.runner/reach-graph`): `seon.test.runner/reach-node` merges `:seon.fn/references` (quoted symbols, kept so a changed symbol that is resolved at run time changes a test's key) with `:seon.fn/calls` into `:seon.test.runner/out`, so every quoted effect owner is an edge: `seon.test/run-request` quotes `seon.effect/request*`, `seon.fn/request-symbol` quotes `seon.effect/request!`, `seon.effect/dispatch` names every tool, and 3,339 reference-only edges join the reach of nearly every test through `seon.test/run`; building the one symbol as c5eb7b814 did for `seon.test/task` still leaves 2,699 (checked on the check JVM and on default's graph with both edges removed). Quoted references must stay in the key, so the change is to the question, not the analysis: add `:seon.test.runner/calls` to `seon.test.runner/reach-node` and a `seon.test.runner/tests-calling` (reverse walk over call edges only, cached like `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching`), and let `seon.test/run-request` ask it for `seon.effect/request*`. Decide first whether a member that reaches an effect only through a runtime-resolved symbol (an interpreted form naming a tool) still needs its attempt context: the call-edge answer is 21 tests, and no stored run records which members executed an effect. Written red proof, not applied: `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/quoted-reach-calls-only.patch` (its deftest asserts a test running a request is not in the effect reach; the patch also holds the insufficient symbol-building edit, drop it). Measure: member cost with and without the attempt context on one fast member."}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-run-refused-once-with-active-connections-on-store-deletion"
             :plan "Suspected flake, to reproduce before anyone designs a fix: one `bin/seon check` run refused with Datahike's 'Cannot delete a database with active connections' and an identical retry passed. The deletion is `seon.cluster.store/release-store!`, called from `seon.test/run`'s cleanup; the sibling task for overlay connections is the family. Run the same check at least 5 times, and on a refusal record which connections `datahike.api/connections` still lists (agent overlays, listeners, a flow proc) and their agent ids. If an agent or listener outlives its world, the owner is whoever admitted it (stop graphs before retracting their facts, release before deleting); otherwise record that it did not reproduce. Read `seon.test/run`, `seon.cluster.store/release-store!` and the sibling task `seon.test/a-world-holding-agent-overlays-releases-cleanly`. Result 2026-10-14 (HEAD 1e278c103): not reproduced, 0 of 8 runs of `bin/seon check --test seon.context-test/a-plain-eval-renders-each-must-read-from-its-world` printed the refusal (7 passed in 999 to 1,227 ms; the eighth failed in classpath resolution, see `seon.operator/concurrent-checks-race-in-clojure-spath-classpath-resolution`); evidence in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/flake-hunt.md` #Suspect 2: active-connections refusal, per run. The 8 runs used a small test, not the full batch in which it was observed, so close this only after a run of that batch also fails to reproduce."}
