 #:seon.task{:name "test-test-reds-reproduce-alone"
             :plan "The four `seon.test-test` vars `seon.test-test/admission-refuses-stale-program-and-records-empty-selection`, `seon.test-test/one-interpreted-test-run-allocates-a-bounded-multiple-of-its-body`, `seon.test-test/recording-distinguishes-run-replay-from-a-new-event` and `seon.test-test/resolution-follows-admitted-source-and-acquisition` are red at HEAD 127fe54f6 both in the namespace and alone. The premise that they pass alone did not reproduce (flake-hunt, 3 alone runs each, `bin/seon check --at HEAD --test VAR`). Alone: admission and recording and resolution are red 3 of 3, one-interpreted is red 1 of 3. So no earlier var in the namespace is needed to break them; the namespace-run reds match the alone reds in message. Causes measured. admission: its valid admit is refused with `seon.test/program-mismatch` against the stored run's `seon.test.runner/program-digest` value, which is the typed unavailable refusal (the fixture value has no commit, basis-t 536870933), so the test's fixture is not a committed value. recording: the refused read's type is `seon.db.write/validation-refusal` where the test expects `seon.db/invalid-read-error` at the read it stubs with `with-redefs` on `db/pull`. resolution: the evaluation returned no `seon.program/row`, so `seon.program/declaration-row` refuses a nil event at the test body. one-interpreted: allocates 100129480 and 100168648 bytes against a 100000000 bound, a margin of a few percent, so it is an allocation-bound flake, not shared state. Done when each named var is green alone and in the namespace, each cause fixed at its producer (the fixture's committed value, the read refusal's writer, the evaluation's row, the measured allocation), never by raising the bound."}
